1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,030
Imagine you are a programmer.

2
00:00:02,940 --> 00:00:04,910
Maybe you have been one for twenty years.

3
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:07,270
You know what a race condition is.

4
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:09,310
You know why NULL is evil.

5
00:00:10,081 --> 00:00:12,341
And you have spent part of
your life arguing about whether

6
00:00:12,421 --> 00:00:15,341
the opening brace belongs on
the same line as the if...

7
00:00:15,421 --> 00:00:18,091
or on its own line underneath.

8
00:00:18,996 --> 00:00:20,746
Then one day you come to work.

9
00:00:21,196 --> 00:00:22,686
You open your editor.

10
00:00:23,536 --> 00:00:26,446
And you realise there is nothing
in there left for you to write.

11
00:00:27,667 --> 00:00:29,437
Because the machine can do it.

12
00:00:30,127 --> 00:00:34,037
You tell it: build me an API for
user management. It writes it.

13
00:00:34,719 --> 00:00:38,959
Add authentication. It writes
it. Add tests. It writes them.

14
00:00:39,039 --> 00:00:41,409
Fix this bug. It fixes it.

15
00:00:42,073 --> 00:00:43,603
And you review the result.

16
00:00:44,579 --> 00:00:47,209
Congratulations. You
have become a manager.

17
00:00:48,019 --> 00:00:49,689
Just without the manager's salary.

18
00:00:51,008 --> 00:00:53,418
And suddenly you are leading
a team of programmers.

19
00:00:54,288 --> 00:00:55,678
Not one of them is human.

20
00:00:57,768 --> 00:00:59,618
But look at what we actually did.

21
00:01:00,408 --> 00:01:02,428
We spent decades teaching programmers to

22
00:01:02,508 --> 00:01:04,918
write code that other
programmers could read.

23
00:01:05,738 --> 00:01:09,868
And now we are building machines that
write code programmers may never read.

24
00:01:10,613 --> 00:01:13,283
So the question is not
whether you still have a job.

25
00:01:14,053 --> 00:01:16,843
The question is what all of
that readability was for.

26
00:01:19,623 --> 00:01:22,813
Start with the thing programmers
say whenever this comes up.

27
00:01:23,263 --> 00:01:25,613
Programming has always been automated.

28
00:01:26,633 --> 00:01:27,983
And that part is true.

29
00:01:28,793 --> 00:01:34,003
The first programmers worked directly in
machine instructions. Then came assembly.

30
00:01:34,653 --> 00:01:36,783
Then languages you could almost read.

31
00:01:37,653 --> 00:01:40,923
Every time, a piece of the work
moved from the person to the tool.

32
00:01:41,670 --> 00:01:45,230
On the 10th of November,
1954, a group at IBM

33
00:01:45,310 --> 00:01:48,180
published a report about a
system they were building.

34
00:01:48,790 --> 00:01:50,600
They called it FORTRAN.

35
00:01:51,325 --> 00:01:52,745
Page two says this.

36
00:01:52,825 --> 00:01:56,635
"Since FORTRAN should virtually
eliminate coding and debugging..."

37
00:01:57,025 --> 00:01:58,805
And the sentence goes
on to promise the work

38
00:01:58,885 --> 00:02:01,415
will cost less than half
of what it cost before.

39
00:02:02,967 --> 00:02:05,597
FORTRAN arrived two
and a half years later.

40
00:02:06,287 --> 00:02:07,937
Coding was not eliminated.

41
00:02:08,667 --> 00:02:10,477
The programmers wrote FORTRAN.

42
00:02:11,510 --> 00:02:16,020
In 1959, a committee sat down to
design a language for business.

43
00:02:16,910 --> 00:02:18,660
They agreed on two things.

44
00:02:18,870 --> 00:02:21,820
That more people should be
able to program at all...

45
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:25,620
and that the language should use
as much plain English as possible.

46
00:02:26,509 --> 00:02:28,509
That was a design goal, not a side effect.

47
00:02:28,589 --> 00:02:34,299
COBOL was shaped around what a human
could read. Hold on to that one.

48
00:02:34,951 --> 00:02:39,061
Then, in 1982, a man called
James Martin published a book.

49
00:02:39,811 --> 00:02:41,941
It is worth hearing the title in full.

50
00:02:42,631 --> 00:02:45,261
"Application Development
Without Programmers."

51
00:02:46,187 --> 00:02:47,637
It was not about robots.

52
00:02:48,567 --> 00:02:51,817
It was about report generators
and application generators.

53
00:02:52,907 --> 00:02:56,037
And the promise was exactly the one
you have been hearing this year.

54
00:02:58,517 --> 00:03:00,667
So here is the obvious question.

55
00:03:01,077 --> 00:03:03,887
After all of that, how
many programmers were left?

56
00:03:04,759 --> 00:03:06,449
More of them. Every time.

57
00:03:06,919 --> 00:03:10,169
In 1980, the United
States had more than three

58
00:03:10,249 --> 00:03:12,669
hundred thousand computer
programming jobs.

59
00:03:13,239 --> 00:03:16,994
By the early 2000s, more
than seven hundred thousand.

60
00:03:17,443 --> 00:03:19,673
But watch what happened
to the word programmer.

61
00:03:20,983 --> 00:03:23,943
In 2003, the American labour survey added

62
00:03:24,023 --> 00:03:27,053
a new job title, right
next to the old one.

63
00:03:27,717 --> 00:03:30,907
Computer programmer.
And software engineer.

64
00:03:31,617 --> 00:03:34,367
Today that second one is
called software developer.

65
00:03:35,455 --> 00:03:37,322
Right now, about three hundred and eighty

66
00:03:37,402 --> 00:03:39,965
thousand Americans are
computer programmers.

67
00:03:41,095 --> 00:03:43,925
And about two and a quarter
million are software developers.

68
00:03:45,003 --> 00:03:47,633
That is six developers
for every one programmer.

69
00:03:48,263 --> 00:03:51,673
Almost nobody doing this work
is still called a programmer.

70
00:03:52,568 --> 00:03:55,958
The job did not disappear. It got renamed.

71
00:03:58,828 --> 00:04:02,658
And that is the shape of the thing
that is happening now, except faster.

72
00:04:03,596 --> 00:04:06,166
Because every earlier step
had one thing in common.

73
00:04:06,316 --> 00:04:07,696
You were still programming.

74
00:04:07,776 --> 00:04:09,806
Just further from the metal.

75
00:04:10,556 --> 00:04:12,156
Assembly to C: still a program.

76
00:04:12,236 --> 00:04:15,206
C to Python: still a program.

77
00:04:16,050 --> 00:04:20,380
Not this time. You describe what
you want in ordinary sentences.

78
00:04:21,290 --> 00:04:24,520
I need a system where users
can change their email address.

79
00:04:25,470 --> 00:04:27,440
The new address must be verified.

80
00:04:28,290 --> 00:04:30,500
Until then, the old one stays active.

81
00:04:31,258 --> 00:04:34,318
And out comes the database
change, the backend, the

82
00:04:34,398 --> 00:04:37,228
API, the front end, the
tests, the documentation.

83
00:04:38,698 --> 00:04:40,168
And you have not written a line.

84
00:04:40,892 --> 00:04:42,982
That is not a higher-level language.

85
00:04:43,652 --> 00:04:45,642
It is a different kind of thing entirely.

86
00:04:48,302 --> 00:04:50,202
And at that point it stops
being a philosophical

87
00:04:50,282 --> 00:04:53,272
question and becomes an arithmetic one.

88
00:04:54,030 --> 00:04:56,230
If one programmer with
these tools does the work

89
00:04:56,310 --> 00:04:59,400
that used to take five,
a company will use that.

90
00:05:00,110 --> 00:05:02,360
The company does not have to be evil.

91
00:05:03,010 --> 00:05:05,460
It does not even have to
want to let anyone go.

92
00:05:06,130 --> 00:05:09,000
It just needs a competitor
who is faster and cheaper.

93
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:13,520
So the centre of gravity moves.

94
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:15,920
From writing to instructing.

95
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,410
From implementation to review.

96
00:05:18,680 --> 00:05:20,830
From programming to architecture.

97
00:05:21,020 --> 00:05:24,790
From writing the code to managing
the thing that writes it.

98
00:05:25,485 --> 00:05:30,925
Which is strange, because that path
already existed. Team leader. Architect.

99
00:05:31,005 --> 00:05:32,735
Engineering manager.

100
00:05:33,564 --> 00:05:35,334
Some programmers wanted it.

101
00:05:36,044 --> 00:05:37,554
Plenty of them did not.

102
00:05:37,984 --> 00:05:42,394
They did not want meetings or budgets
or deciding who takes leave next week.

103
00:05:43,115 --> 00:05:45,355
They wanted to sit down,
look at something, and

104
00:05:45,435 --> 00:05:48,605
think: how on earth am I
going to make this work?

105
00:05:49,397 --> 00:05:53,057
And now the work is moving them
in exactly the opposite direction.

106
00:05:53,137 --> 00:05:55,487
Not because they got older.

107
00:05:55,977 --> 00:05:57,747
Because the craft got automated.

108
00:06:00,607 --> 00:06:02,397
There is a quieter cost, too.

109
00:06:03,107 --> 00:06:07,037
When you solve it yourself, you have
to think about it. You try something.

110
00:06:07,787 --> 00:06:09,617
You get it wrong. You take it apart.

111
00:06:10,565 --> 00:06:14,515
And a year later you remember.
Oh. I have had this before.

112
00:06:15,314 --> 00:06:18,164
When the machine solves it,
the job takes ten minutes.

113
00:06:19,094 --> 00:06:23,544
And a week later you open your own project
and think: why did I do it this way?

114
00:06:24,265 --> 00:06:26,495
And the honest answer is: I don't know.

115
00:06:26,905 --> 00:06:28,255
AI wrote it.

116
00:06:28,971 --> 00:06:31,161
Which is a very odd place to end up.

117
00:06:31,951 --> 00:06:35,501
More productive programmers, slowly
becoming less able to program.

118
00:06:37,064 --> 00:06:40,894
If you'd like to see where the next
question takes us, consider subscribing.

119
00:06:41,764 --> 00:06:43,174
It really helps the channel.

120
00:06:44,367 --> 00:06:49,037
And then there are the things that used
to matter enormously. Coding style.

121
00:06:49,905 --> 00:06:53,125
We had entire arguments about
where to put a curly brace.

122
00:06:53,205 --> 00:06:55,315
Tabs or spaces.

123
00:06:55,485 --> 00:06:57,025
Two spaces or four.

124
00:06:57,105 --> 00:06:59,215
CamelCase or snake_case.

125
00:07:00,631 --> 00:07:03,381
Arguments that outlasted
the code they were about.

126
00:07:04,247 --> 00:07:06,987
But if a machine writes
thousands of lines, and people

127
00:07:07,067 --> 00:07:10,237
barely read them, and nobody
will maintain them by hand...

128
00:07:10,867 --> 00:07:12,837
how much does the brace matter?

129
00:07:13,525 --> 00:07:16,235
Coding style becomes an
implementation detail.

130
00:07:16,345 --> 00:07:18,895
Something that matters to the machine...

131
00:07:19,525 --> 00:07:21,235
and less and less to the human.

132
00:07:23,575 --> 00:07:26,125
Now think about spaceships
in science fiction.

133
00:07:27,106 --> 00:07:31,556
The ship has a computer that runs
everything. Engines. Sensors.

134
00:07:31,686 --> 00:07:32,886
Life support.

135
00:07:33,497 --> 00:07:36,847
And there is a medical bay,
an engineering deck, a bridge.

136
00:07:37,711 --> 00:07:39,431
What it does not have
is a room with a sign

137
00:07:39,511 --> 00:07:42,281
on the door saying: software engineering.

138
00:07:43,268 --> 00:07:46,518
Nobody on the bridge is
arguing about library versions.

139
00:07:47,008 --> 00:07:50,798
Nobody says life support is
down because of a null pointer.

140
00:07:51,729 --> 00:07:55,539
The captain says: analyse the
anomaly. And it gets analysed.

141
00:07:57,011 --> 00:08:00,481
For decades that looked like the part
the writers had not thought about.

142
00:08:01,111 --> 00:08:03,461
It may turn out to be
the part they got right.

143
00:08:06,131 --> 00:08:08,221
Because the problem does not go away.

144
00:08:08,931 --> 00:08:11,721
Somebody still has to know
what the system should do.

145
00:08:12,458 --> 00:08:13,848
What it must never do.

146
00:08:14,278 --> 00:08:16,448
What the security requirements are.

147
00:08:16,558 --> 00:08:18,848
What data it is allowed to touch.

148
00:08:19,098 --> 00:08:21,248
What happens when it fails.

149
00:08:22,203 --> 00:08:26,053
And above all, somebody has to answer
the question underneath all of those.

150
00:08:27,063 --> 00:08:28,893
How do we know it built the right thing?

151
00:08:29,597 --> 00:08:32,547
That may turn out to be harder
than writing the code was.

152
00:08:35,457 --> 00:08:36,987
And it leads somewhere odd.

153
00:08:37,617 --> 00:08:39,187
Why would a machine write Python?

154
00:08:40,163 --> 00:08:43,483
Python, C sharp, PHP —
every one of them was

155
00:08:43,563 --> 00:08:46,413
designed so that a person
could read and write it.

156
00:08:47,107 --> 00:08:48,937
A machine does not need that.

157
00:08:49,667 --> 00:08:53,117
What it needs is a precise description
of the thing it has to build.

158
00:08:54,007 --> 00:08:57,597
What must always be true.
What must never happen.

159
00:08:58,323 --> 00:09:02,633
And if that is what we end up writing,
then the thing we keep is not the diff.

160
00:09:03,454 --> 00:09:05,034
Not 37 changed files.

161
00:09:05,114 --> 00:09:12,484
Not 4,821 lines added and 1,734 removed.

162
00:09:13,193 --> 00:09:18,573
The thing we keep is the specification.
The test scenarios. The security rules.

163
00:09:18,653 --> 00:09:19,853
The data model.

164
00:09:20,395 --> 00:09:22,545
And the code becomes
a means of production.

165
00:09:23,495 --> 00:09:25,885
Nobody asks how the
factory made the screw.

166
00:09:26,555 --> 00:09:28,665
They ask whether the screw meets the spec.

167
00:09:29,519 --> 00:09:31,949
Which makes the specification
the source code.

168
00:09:32,379 --> 00:09:35,649
And the code underneath it
becomes what assembly is today.

169
00:09:36,099 --> 00:09:39,529
Something that still runs,
and that almost nobody reads.

170
00:09:42,159 --> 00:09:45,609
So perhaps the sentence everybody
says is the wrong sentence.

171
00:09:46,439 --> 00:09:48,489
Not: this will replace programmers.

172
00:09:49,510 --> 00:09:51,670
Closer to it would be: this will replace

173
00:09:51,750 --> 00:09:54,500
programming as the main
thing programmers do.

174
00:09:55,195 --> 00:09:57,245
The programmers may not go anywhere.

175
00:09:57,455 --> 00:09:59,885
The programming may go out of their jobs.

176
00:10:02,645 --> 00:10:05,215
Which brings us back to the
question from the start.

177
00:10:06,065 --> 00:10:07,615
What was the readability for?

178
00:10:08,439 --> 00:10:10,249
All those English-like keywords.

179
00:10:10,759 --> 00:10:13,109
All those arguments about braces.

180
00:10:13,959 --> 00:10:16,069
The machine never needed any of it.

181
00:10:16,731 --> 00:10:17,931
It was for us.

182
00:10:18,011 --> 00:10:22,641
So that a person could pick up something
somebody else had made, and understand it.

183
00:10:25,861 --> 00:10:27,371
Maybe you are a programmer.

184
00:10:28,001 --> 00:10:29,411
Maybe you were one, once.

185
00:10:30,311 --> 00:10:32,291
And maybe, in a while,
someone young will find

186
00:10:32,371 --> 00:10:35,511
one of these old
repositories and ask: wait.

187
00:10:35,591 --> 00:10:37,501
You wrote these yourselves?

188
00:10:38,559 --> 00:10:39,759
Yes.

189
00:10:40,361 --> 00:10:42,351
Did you write the boring parts too?

190
00:10:43,188 --> 00:10:44,388
Yes, everything.

191
00:10:45,375 --> 00:10:46,575
Why?

192
00:10:48,249 --> 00:10:51,099
And the honest answer is:
because we enjoyed it.
